1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,543
In 1935,

2
00:00:00,543 --> 00:00:03,123
in the depths of the Great Depression,

3
00:00:03,123 --> 00:00:07,468
a twenty-five-year-old secretary named Grace Groner went to work

4
00:00:07,468 --> 00:00:11,270
at Abbott Laboratories in North Chicago and bought three

5
00:00:11,270 --> 00:00:15,139
specially issued shares of company stock at sixty dollars

6
00:00:15,139 --> 00:00:15,614
apiece.

7
00:00:15,614 --> 00:00:19,484
Her entire capital outlay was one hundred eighty dollars.

8
00:00:19,484 --> 00:00:22,132
Over the next seven and a half decades,

9
00:00:22,132 --> 00:00:25,866
Groner worked forty-three years as an Abbott secretary,

10
00:00:25,866 --> 00:00:27,699
never bought another share,

11
00:00:27,699 --> 00:00:29,260
never traded an option,

12
00:00:29,260 --> 00:00:31,840
and never borrowed a dollar on margin.

13
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:35,234
She lived happily in a tiny one-bedroom cottage in

14
00:00:35,234 --> 00:00:36,049
Lake Forest,

15
00:00:36,049 --> 00:00:36,660
Illinois,

16
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:38,153
shopped rummage sales,

17
00:00:38,153 --> 00:00:40,258
walked instead of owning a car,

18
00:00:40,258 --> 00:00:43,652
and reinvested every dividend across stock splits.

19
00:00:43,652 --> 00:00:46,979
When she died in January 2010 at age one hundred,

20
00:00:46,979 --> 00:00:50,238
her three shares exceeded seven million dollars,

21
00:00:50,238 --> 00:00:53,700
funding student fellowships at Lake Forest College.

22
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:57,998
Contrast Grace Groner's seventy-five-year hold with the most expensive

23
00:00:57,998 --> 00:01:00,576
twelve-day exit in Silicon Valley history.

24
00:01:00,576 --> 00:01:01,251
On April 1,

25
00:01:01,251 --> 00:01:01,558
1976,

26
00:01:01,558 --> 00:01:05,672
forty-one-year-old draftsman Ronald Wayne sat at his typewriter and

27
00:01:05,672 --> 00:01:09,724
drafted Apple Computer's original three-page partnership contract,

28
00:01:09,724 --> 00:01:12,855
taking a ten percent stake alongside Steve Jobs and

29
00:01:12,855 --> 00:01:15,372
Steve Wozniak at forty-five percent each.

30
00:01:15,372 --> 00:01:16,477
Twelve days later,

31
00:01:16,477 --> 00:01:17,214
on April 12,

32
00:01:17,214 --> 00:01:20,161
Wayne sold his entire ten percent back for eight

33
00:01:20,161 --> 00:01:21,143
hundred dollars,

34
00:01:21,143 --> 00:01:24,520
accepting fifteen hundred more later that year to waive

35
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:25,625
all future claims.

36
00:01:25,625 --> 00:01:28,019
Commentators mock Wayne for impatience,

37
00:01:28,019 --> 00:01:30,352
yet the legal record shows cold logic:

38
00:01:30,352 --> 00:01:35,018
early Apple was a general partnership carrying unlimited personal liability.

39
00:01:35,018 --> 00:01:38,579
When Jobs took credit obligations to fill a fifty-computer

40
00:01:38,579 --> 00:01:39,561
Byte Shop order,

41
00:01:39,561 --> 00:01:44,166
broke twenty-somethings Jobs and Wozniak had nothing creditors could seize,

42
00:01:44,166 --> 00:01:48,157
whereas Wayne owned personal savings and carried painful memories

43
00:01:48,157 --> 00:01:50,981
of paying off partner debts from a failed 1971

44
00:01:50,981 --> 00:01:52,270
slot-machine venture.

45
00:01:52,270 --> 00:01:55,136
Putting Groner and Wayne side by side exposes a

46
00:01:55,136 --> 00:01:57,454
rule financial media routinely misses:

47
00:01:57,454 --> 00:02:01,662
investing temperament is downstream of legal and cash-flow structure.

48
00:02:01,662 --> 00:02:04,467
Grace Groner did not possess superhuman nerve;

49
00:02:04,467 --> 00:02:07,029
she possessed a bulletproof balance sheet.

50
00:02:07,029 --> 00:02:10,932
Corporate limited liability capped her worst conceivable loss at

51
00:02:10,932 --> 00:02:12,579
one hundred eighty dollars,

52
00:02:12,579 --> 00:02:15,689
and her frugal Lake Forest budget never depended on

53
00:02:15,689 --> 00:02:18,921
selling shares to pay property taxes or utility bills

54
00:02:18,921 --> 00:02:21,117
across thirteen American recessions.

55
00:02:21,117 --> 00:02:24,471
Whenever a solo founder signs personal debt guarantees,

56
00:02:24,471 --> 00:02:27,276
or an investor buys equities on margin or with

57
00:02:27,276 --> 00:02:28,801
next year's tuition cash,

58
00:02:28,801 --> 00:02:32,948
ordinary thirty-percent drawdowns turn into existential emergencies,

59
00:02:32,948 --> 00:02:36,363
forcing rational people out of great assets right before

60
00:02:36,363 --> 00:02:38,010
the power law bends upward.

61
00:02:38,010 --> 00:02:40,256
Even after you cap legal downside,

62
00:02:40,256 --> 00:02:44,021
a second psychological trap sabotages multi-decade holds.

63
00:02:44,021 --> 00:02:44,550
In 1963,

64
00:02:44,550 --> 00:02:48,249
MIT economist Paul Samuelson offered a lunch colleague a

65
00:02:48,249 --> 00:02:49,042
clean wager:

66
00:02:49,042 --> 00:02:50,165
flip a fair coin,

67
00:02:50,165 --> 00:02:52,345
win two hundred dollars on heads,

68
00:02:52,345 --> 00:02:54,789
or lose one hundred dollars on tails.

69
00:02:54,789 --> 00:02:58,356
His colleague turned down a single toss because losing

70
00:02:58,356 --> 00:03:02,121
one hundred dollars stung harder than winning two hundred

71
00:03:02,121 --> 00:03:02,782
felt good,

72
00:03:02,782 --> 00:03:06,745
yet offered to play if Samuelson promised one hundred flips!

73
00:03:06,745 --> 00:03:07,274
In 1995,

74
00:03:07,274 --> 00:03:11,700
behavioral economists Shlomo Benartzi and Richard Thaler named this

75
00:03:11,700 --> 00:03:13,747
mechanism Myopic Loss Aversion.

76
00:03:13,747 --> 00:03:17,513
Checking a brokerage app every afternoon slices a winning

77
00:03:17,513 --> 00:03:20,882
thirty-year marathon into thousands of coin tosses,

78
00:03:20,882 --> 00:03:24,383
flooding your nervous system with loss pain until you

79
00:03:24,383 --> 00:03:25,770
capitulate into cash.

80
00:03:25,770 --> 00:03:29,508
Watch how removing leverage and silencing daily quotes changed

81
00:03:29,508 --> 00:03:32,281
Grace Groner's relationship with bear markets.

82
00:03:32,281 --> 00:03:35,537
Because she never stared at flashing tickers and never

83
00:03:35,537 --> 00:03:36,803
faced a margin clerk,

84
00:03:36,803 --> 00:03:38,189
market crashes in 1937,

85
00:03:38,189 --> 00:03:38,491
1974,

86
00:03:38,491 --> 00:03:38,792
1987,

87
00:03:38,792 --> 00:03:42,289
and 2008 required zero decisions from her cottage kitchen.

88
00:03:42,289 --> 00:03:45,303
Every time panic pushed Abbott's share price down,

89
00:03:45,303 --> 00:03:50,187
her reinvested quarterly dividends automatically purchased more fractional shares

90
00:03:50,187 --> 00:03:51,634
at depressed valuations.

91
00:03:51,634 --> 00:03:53,081
Over seventy-five years,

92
00:03:53,081 --> 00:03:57,180
splits and reinvested payouts turned three initial certificates into

93
00:03:57,180 --> 00:03:59,471
more than one hundred thousand shares.

94
00:03:59,471 --> 00:04:03,691
She outperformed frantic professionals not by predicting macro cycles,

95
00:04:03,691 --> 00:04:06,947
but by designing a quiet life where uninterrupted time

96
00:04:06,947 --> 00:04:09,660
did ninety-nine percent of the heavy lifting.

97
00:04:09,660 --> 00:04:13,235
Build Grace Groner's staying power into your finances and

98
00:04:13,235 --> 00:04:16,621
solo business this week with three structural shields.

99
00:04:16,621 --> 00:04:16,997
First,

100
00:04:16,997 --> 00:04:19,129
install a hard liability firewall:

101
00:04:19,129 --> 00:04:23,268
house every business experiment inside a limited-liability entity,

102
00:04:23,268 --> 00:04:25,212
avoid personal debt guarantees,

103
00:04:25,212 --> 00:04:28,473
and run zero margin loans in your investment account

104
00:04:28,473 --> 00:04:31,672
so nobody can force a twelve-day Ronald Wayne exit.

105
00:04:31,672 --> 00:04:32,110
Second,

106
00:04:32,110 --> 00:04:35,936
maintain twelve to twenty-four months of lean household bills

107
00:04:35,936 --> 00:04:39,824
in safe Treasury reserves completely separate from your equity

108
00:04:39,824 --> 00:04:40,451
portfolio.

109
00:04:40,451 --> 00:04:40,827
Third,

110
00:04:40,827 --> 00:04:43,336
turn on automatic dividend reinvestment,

111
00:04:43,336 --> 00:04:46,283
strip live stock widgets off your phone screen,

112
00:04:46,283 --> 00:04:49,858
and inspect long-term holdings on a calm written schedule

113
00:04:49,858 --> 00:04:51,300
just four times a year.
